Description: Prefer rpi-hevc-dec's multi-planar SAND formats (NV12MT_COL128)
 Raspberry Pi's HEVC decoder (/dev/video19) offers two families of CAPTURE
 formats on kernels >= 6.18:
 .
  - 'Nc12' / 'Nc30' (V4L2_PIX_FMT_NV12MT_COL128 / NV12MT_10_COL128):
    multi-planar, luma and chroma in separate buffers. Listed first by the
    driver; this is the upstream format and the one Raspberry Pi's FFmpeg
    and GStreamer pick by default.
  - 'NC12' / 'NC30' (legacy single-planar NV12_COL128 / NV12_10_COL128).
 .
 The slice bit_size semantics of the multi-planar formats are the same on
 every kernel, while the legacy formats changed meaning on some kernels
 (rpt 6.18.29, Ubuntu linux-raspi 7.0), which broke HEVC there (issue #81).
 Teach chromium about the multi-planar formats and prefer them; patch 0018
 then picks bit_size from the negotiated format. Kernels without them
 (rpi 6.12.y) keep using NC12/NC30 unchanged.
 .
 Layout: two V4L2 planes -> two dma-bufs, modifier BROADCOM_SAND128. SAND128
 consumers (Mesa v3d via EGL/GBM, KMS) take the column height in the pitch
 field, so the plane strides are overridden to height (luma) and height / 2
 (chroma), matching what Raspberry Pi's FFmpeg exports. The GBM/EGL import
 patches (0006, 0011, 0015, 0017, 0021) key off the modifier, not the
 fourcc, so they apply unchanged.
Forwarded: not-needed
Author: Steve Slivinski <stesli@github.local>
Last-Update: 2026-10-10
